					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/modernmajorgeneral" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(274283); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=274283&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607200789" alt="ModernMajorGeneral" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=274283)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/modernmajorgeneral" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(274283); return false;" >ModernMajorGeneral</a></span> <span class="odate time_1535373600 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">27 Aug 2018 12:40</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3933430">
									

<p>Mixed feelings about this. I like the flower character, her development through the article and the atmosphere created, like the Edith Piaf references. I don't really like the 'wow thanks I'm cured' approach to mental illness (to clarify, I don't think this is an <em>offensive</em> portrayal of mental illness as much as it is a simplistic one which leaves the narrative potential of the subject unexplored) or the letter. I also was expecting this to have a horror twist, which left me feeling a bit unsatisfied, though I don't think that would have improved the article as such.</p>
<p>My dislike of the letter is very pedantic, but stems from it feeling out of place. Assuming this letter was found in the French garden where the SCP was discovered, it's written in English (presumably, without a note it was translated), by someone with an un-French-sounding name and by someone who appears not to be completely familiar with La Vie en Rose, which is like the most famous French song of all time. Of course all of these things are completely possible in a letter written by someone who lives in France, but taken together make the author's non-French voice (presumably) too obvious. I also don't like this trend I've seen of using a cursive font for handwritten letters. No government agency changes their documents to a cursive font to indicate handwriting - they would either just transcribe the letter or take a photograph of it. The alternative, that it's meant to improve immersion for the reader by creating the illusion of handwriting, also makes no sense, as it's clearly a computer generated font.</p>
<p>Novoted.</p>

							</div>

								
					
