														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3934716">
									

<p>That's understandable. In the early drafting stages, we actually had Mia as an American woman who flew to France just to check out the garden, the note is the only part that we've left relatively unchanged. In my mind, I like to think that Maria was born from an American father, justifying Mia's ability to speak English. I might write a tale about that some time.</p>
<p>In other words, the letter was meant to add depth to the Queen/Mia and was there to act as an early emotional punch; it was originally the ending part of this when we were drafting. The formatting as well was meant to act as a sort of 'pseudo-picture' as well, which was justified by the 'document attached.' I understand the issue you might have with this, but I thought that it would help the reader put themselves in the shoes of the daughter reading the actual note for the first time.</p>
<p>It's unfortunate that you disliked some aspects of this, and I really hope you give the piece a second chance.</p>
<p><em>EDIT: In regard of the expected horror twist, I feel that would just be a stain on what we were going for. This was all around made to try and envoke an emotional reaction from the reader.</em></p>

